"Not All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence:Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1)\nciteturn41232view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1\",\"lineno\":210}); Total lines: 472\nL151: ##### Candidate-blind independent solving.\nL152: \nL153: When $z(q)=1$, we ask three heterogeneous solvers to solve the original question. Each solver receives only the question and its answer options and answers the question independently. No solver sees $a_{E}(q)$, $a_{F}(q)$, the reasoning traces behind them, or another solver’s output. Each valid response is normalized to a task-specific answer representation $v_{k}$.\nL154: ##### Answer-level arbitration.\nL155: \nL156: Let $\\mathcal{V}$ denote the indices of solvers with valid responses. We count their support for $a_{E}$ and $a_{F}$:\nL157: \nL158:  | $$n_{E}=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\mathbf{1}[v_{k}=a_{E}],\\qquad n_{F}=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\mathbf{1}[v_{k}=a_{F}].$$  |\nL159: \nL160: If at least two solvers agree on an answer outside $\\{a_{E},a_{F}\\}$, we return that answer. Otherwise, we return $a_{E}$ when $n_{E}>n_{F}$ and $a_{F}$ when $n_{F}>n_{E}$.\nL161: ### 3.1 Theoretical Analysis\nL162: \nL163: We explain why heterogeneous solvers and candidate-blind solving help through a single quantity, the truth margin. Formal statements and proofs are in Appendix cite49†B .\nL164: ##### Setup.\nL165: \nL166: For a question with gold answer $y^{\\star}$ and a panel $S$ of $n$ solvers with average accuracy $\\bar{p}_{S}$, let $\\kappa_{S}\\in[0,1]$ be the fraction of the panel’s expected wrong votes that fall on its single most popular wrong answer, and define\nL167: \nL168:  | $$\\Delta_{S}=\\bar{p}_{S}-\\kappa_{S}\\,(1-\\bar{p}_{S}).$$  |  | (1)\nL169: $\\Delta_{S}$ is the expected vote share of $y^{\\star}$ minus that of the strongest wrong answer. The definition encodes an asymmetry: correct votes always coincide on $y^{\\star}$, whereas wrong votes defeat the truth only if they also coincide on the same wrong answer.\nL170: ###### Proposition 1 (informal; Proposition cite81†B.1 ).\nL171: \nL172: Under independent voting, plurality is governed by the truth margin $\\Delta_{S}$, the expected vote share of the gold answer relative to the strongest wrong alternative. If $\\Delta_{S}>0$, increasing the number of independent votes concentrates the outcome on the gold answer, exponentially fast in $n$; if $\\Delta_{S}<0$, additional votes instead reinforce the dominant error mode.\nL173: Repeated samples from the same model preserve the answer distribution and therefore the sign of $\\Delta_{S}$: for a homogeneous panel, $\\bar{p}_{S}$ is the model’s own accuracy and $\\kappa_{S}$ is exactly the wrong-answer concentration $C_{q}$ of Section cite8†2.1 , and neither depends on the sampling budget.\nL174: Only a change of solver population can change the sign of the margin, so the benefit of heterogeneity is not diversity per se but a reduction of shared error modes; Section cite30†4.3.2 tests this prediction.\nL175: ###### Proposition 2 (informal; Proposition cite82†B.2 ).\nL176: \nL177: Candidate exposure systematically shifts a solver’s answer distribution toward the exposed candidate. If the exposed candidate is correct, this shift increases the truth margin. If it is incorrect, the same shift decreases the margin against that wrong answer and can reverse its sign. Candidate-blind solving removes this pre-aggregation shift.\nL178: Exposure amplifies the candidate being shown, not necessarily the truth, so communication helps only when solvers can reliably distinguish correct from incorrect shared answers, which is exactly what Section cite10†2.3 calls into question. Dia therefore obtains each solver’s judgment before revealing any candidate, so the evidence entering aggregation is not conditioned on the alternatives being arbitrated.\nL179: ## 4 Experiments\nL180: \nL181: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL182: ##### Datasets.\nL183: We evaluate on seven benchmarks across four reasoning domains. MMLU-Pro (cite83†Wang et al., 2024 ) and GPQA-Diamond (cite84†Rein et al., 2024 ) cover knowledge-intensive reasoning. DetectBench (cite85†Gu et al., 2024 ), MuSiQue (cite75†Trivedi et al., 2022 ), and 2WikiMultiHopQA (cite86†Ho et al., 2020 ) cover contextual and multi-hop reasoning.\nL184: We use MATH (cite87†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ), restricted to problems of difficulty levels 3 to 5, for mathematical reasoning and MuSR (cite88†Sprague et al., 2024 ) for narrative or soft reasoning. Dataset statistics and sampling details are provided in Appendix cite39†A.1 .\nL185: ##### Baselines.\nL186: We compare Dia with seven baselines, grouped by the kind of intervention each applies against model-specific error bias. ① Standard reasoning. CoT (cite54†Wei et al., 2022 ) lets the base model complete its reasoning and returns the final prediction. ② Adaptive reasoning.\nL187: DEER (cite56†Yang et al., 2026 ) and Dynasor (cite57†Fu et al., 2025 ) use intermediate reasoning signals to decide whether continued reasoning is necessary: DEER relies on confidence at intermediate reasoning states, whereas Dynasor, in its CoT setting, uses the stability of intermediate predictions. Like Dia, both act on signals exposed before reasoning terminates, but they modify the computation of the same model rather than seeking evidence outside it. ③ Multi-agent inference.\nL188: We consider two protocols using the same four-agent pool. MAS-Exchange (cite59†Wu et al., 2024 ; cite60†Li et al., 2023 ) uses fully-connected communication: agents first solve the question independently, observe all peer responses, revise once, and then vote. MAS-Judge (cite72†Wang et al., 2025 ) removes peer-to-peer communication: agents solve independently, after which a centralized judge, instantiated by the base model, observes all responses and selects the final answer. ④ Learning-based debiasing.\nL189: We test whether model-specific error bias can be reduced through parameter updates: SFT trains the base model on its own correct samples and DPO then prefers correct over incorrect samples of the same question (details in Appendix cite44†A.2 ).\nL190: ##### Implementation.\nL191: \nL192: We use Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B as base models. The external solvers are DeepSeek-v4-Flash (cite89†DeepSeek-AI, 2026 ), Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct (cite90†Meta, 2024 ), and GPT-4o-mini (cite91†OpenAI, 2024 ). Following cite56†Yang et al. (2026) , we extract the intermediate-stage prediction at reasoning-transition points when the confidence criterion is met. The final-stage prediction is obtained by letting the same base model complete its reasoning under matched inference settings.\nL193: ##### Metrics.\nL194: \nL195: We report answer accuracy in percent, and all accuracy differences are absolute differences. Token Ratio is the total input and output token usage normalized by the CoT baseline on the same dataset and model.\nL196: ##### Evaluation protocol.\nL197: \nL198: Answers are normalized to option keys before aggregation. The final selected answer is scored against the benchmark gold option. For free-form outputs, the final answer is evaluated by Qwen 3.7-Plus (qwen/qwen3.7-plus), an independent LLM judge, against the benchmark gold answer.\nL199: ### 4.2 Experimental Results\nL200: \nL201: Table 2: Main results across model scales. For each dataset, adjacent columns report accuracy (%) and the total input and output token ratio relative to CoT. Average Acc. and Tok. are the corresponding unweighted arithmetic means across the seven datasets. Bold indicates the highest reported accuracy in each column within each base-model block.\nL202: Models  | Methods     | Knowledge-intensive     | Contextual & Multi-hop     | Mathematical     | Narrative / Soft     | Average\nL203: --- | --- | --- | --- | --